\frametitle{Why is the recall for \mono {experiments} so much higher?}
Maybe citations in \mono{experiments} are easier for the model to resolve?\\
    Consider this example from \cite{dosovitskiy2020image}:
    \begin{lstlisting}
        We transfer the models trained on these dataset to several benchmark tasks: ImageNet on the original validation labels and the cleaned-up ReaL labels (Beyer et al., 2020), CIFAR-10/100 (Krizhevsky, 2009), Oxford-IIIT Pets (Parkhi et al., 2012), and Oxford Flowers-102 (Nilsback & Zisserman, 2008).
    \end{lstlisting}
    \begin{table}[t]
        \begin{center}
            \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
                & \mono{intro+relwork} & \mono{methods} & \mono{experiments} & \mono{conclusion} \\
                \hline
                \hline
                last token in abstract & $0.08$ & $0.12$ & \textbf{0.22} & $0.11$ \\
                \hline
            \end{tabular}}
        \end{center}
        \caption{Fraction of cases where the last token before the citation appears in the abstract of the cited document.}
        \label{tab:last_token}
    \end{table}
